while significant progress has been made  to strengthen the financial system I  believe the Act did not go far enough I  believe the biggest banks are still too  big to fail and continue to pose an  ongoing large risk to our economy  failures of large financial institutions  pose massively asymmetric risk to  society that policymakers must consider  we had a choice in 2008 spend taxpayer  money to stabilize large banks or don't  and potentially trigger many trillions  of addition of additional cost to  society a very crude analogy is that of  a nuclear reactor the cost to society of  letting a reactor meltdown is  astronomical given that costs  governments will do whatever they can to  stabilize the reactor before they lose  control I believe we need to complete  the important work that my colleagues  are doing so that at a minimum we are as  prepared as we can be to deal with an  individual large bank failure but given  the enormous cost that would be  associated with another financial crisis  and the lack of certainty about whether  these new tools would be effective in  dealing with one I believe we must  seriously consider bolder  transformational options breaking up the  large banks into smaller less connected  less important entities turning large  banks into public utilities by forcing  them to hold so much capital they  virtually can't fail with regulation  akin to that of a nuclear power plan  taxing leverage throughout the financial  system to reduce systemic risks wherever  they lie the financial sector has  lobbied hard to preserve its current  structure and thrown up endless  objections to fundamental change and in  the immediate aftermath of the financial  crisis when the dodd-frank act was first  passed the economic outlook was perhaps  too uncertain to take truly bold action  but the economy is stronger now and the  time has come to move past parochial  interests and solve this problem the  risks of not doing so are simply too  great
 

